    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "kuna-hakuna",
        "title": "Kuna and hakuna",
        "body": [
          "**Kuna** means “there is” or “there are”: **Kuna kitanda** (there is a bed). Put **ha-** in front for the negative: **Hakuna maji** (there is no water).",
          "So **Hakuna matata** is simply “there are no problems”: a real, everyday phrase, not just a song."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "yuko-wako",
        "title": "Where people are: yuko, wako",
        "body": [
          "For people, “is (at a place)” is **yuko** for one and **wako** for many: **Mama yuko jikoni** (mum is in the kitchen), **Watoto wako nje** (the children are outside).",
          "For things, you met **iko**: **Chai iko juu ya meza**."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "colours-agree",
        "title": "Colours that change",
        "body": [
          "**Nyekundu**, **nyeupe** and **nyeusi** are describing words, so they take the noun’s prefix: **nyumba nyeupe** (white house), but **mlango mwekundu** (red door) and **kitanda cheusi** (black bed).",
          "**Kijani**, **njano** and **buluu** never change. People often say **rangi ya kijani**, “the colour of leaves”."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["Noun", "Red", "White"],
          ["nyumba", "nyumba nyekundu", "nyumba nyeupe"],
          ["mlango", "mlango mwekundu", "mlango mweupe"],
          ["kitanda", "kitanda chekundu", "kitanda cheupe"]
        ],
        "targetColumns": [0, 1, 2]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Swahili doors",
        "body": [
          "The carved wooden doors of Lamu and Zanzibar’s Stone Town are famous. A family’s door showed its wealth and wishes, carved with chains for security, fish for plenty and lotus flowers for many children."
        ]
      }
    ]
